Every unit on the channel has one callsign, and it does not change during a shift. Dispatch addresses units by callsign only, never by name.

## Structure

A callsign has three parts read in order: detachment, function, and unit number.

| Part | Example | Notes |
| ---- | ------- | ----- |
| Detachment | 3 | Numeric prefix identifying the detachment or zone |
| Function | A | Letter identifying the assignment type |
| Unit number | 12 | Sequential, assigned at start of shift |

Spoken as: three Adam twelve.

## Function letters

| Letter | Assignment |
| :----: | ---------- |
| A | General patrol |
| S | Supervisor |
| K | Canine |
| T | Traffic enforcement |
| D | Detective or plainclothes |
| C | Communications and dispatch |

## Addressing units on the air

<Steps>

<Step title="Call the unit, then identify yourself">
"Three Adam twelve, dispatch." Wait for the unit to acknowledge before continuing with the message.
</Step>

<Step title="Give the traffic">
Once acknowledged, deliver the call: priority, location, nature.
</Step>

<Step title="Confirm the copy">
Do not assume receipt. If a unit does not acknowledge after two attempts, note it and try again after clearing other traffic.
</Step>

</Steps>

<Info>
Spell callsigns phonetically whenever the channel is noisy. The letter is the part that gets lost. See the [Phonetic Alphabet](/phonetics) page for the tables.
</Info>

<Note>
The detachment prefixes and function letters above are a template. Replace them with your own before publishing.
</Note>
